1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,246
A team buys faster servers.

2
00:00:02,246 --> 00:00:04,186
They hire two more developers.

3
00:00:04,186 --> 00:00:05,479
They add more tests,

4
00:00:05,479 --> 00:00:06,708
to run in parallel.

5
00:00:07,258 --> 00:00:10,885
And releases still take three days. Nothing got faster. Why?

6
00:00:11,435 --> 00:00:13,587
Because they improved the wrong thing.

7
00:00:13,587 --> 00:00:17,891
Somewhere in the process is one slow step that sets the pace for everything,

8
00:00:17,891 --> 00:00:19,477
and they never looked at it.

9
00:00:20,027 --> 00:00:21,740
Today: how to find that step.

10
00:00:21,740 --> 00:00:23,276
We call it the bottleneck.

11
00:00:23,276 --> 00:00:24,516
And we will find one,

12
00:00:24,516 --> 00:00:25,284
with numbers,

13
00:00:25,284 --> 00:00:26,406
in a real terminal.

14
00:00:27,956 --> 00:00:29,385
Here is what to expect.

15
00:00:29,935 --> 00:00:32,517
One: what a bottleneck is, and why it matters.

16
00:00:33,067 --> 00:00:36,629
Two: work time versus waiting time, and flow efficiency.

17
00:00:37,179 --> 00:00:40,379
Three: how to find the bottleneck, and the usual suspects.

18
00:00:40,929 --> 00:00:46,263
Four: a live demo. We measure a pipeline, find the slow step, fix it, and measure again.

19
00:00:46,813 --> 00:00:50,162
This is lesson seven of the DevOps fundamentals domain.

20
00:00:52,212 --> 00:00:53,855
First, what is a bottleneck?

21
00:00:54,405 --> 00:00:55,459
Think of a bottle.

22
00:00:55,459 --> 00:00:56,103
It is wide,

23
00:00:56,103 --> 00:00:57,451
but the neck is narrow.

24
00:00:57,451 --> 00:00:59,091
No matter how fast you pour,

25
00:00:59,091 --> 00:01:01,551
the water leaves at the speed of the neck.

26
00:01:02,101 --> 00:01:04,273
A delivery process is the same.

27
00:01:04,273 --> 00:01:06,585
Code flows through steps: review,

28
00:01:06,585 --> 00:01:07,005
build,

29
00:01:07,005 --> 00:01:07,355
test,

30
00:01:07,355 --> 00:01:07,986
approval,

31
00:01:07,986 --> 00:01:08,476
deploy.

32
00:01:08,476 --> 00:01:11,979
The slowest step sets the speed of the whole line.

33
00:01:12,529 --> 00:01:15,568
That means improving any other step changes nothing.

34
00:01:15,568 --> 00:01:17,264
Make the build twice as fast,

35
00:01:17,264 --> 00:01:19,953
and the release is still waiting for approval.

36
00:01:20,503 --> 00:01:24,983
So the first rule of improvement: find the bottleneck before you fix anything.

37
00:01:26,533 --> 00:01:31,290
Here is a surprise. Most of the time in a process is not work. It is waiting.

38
00:01:31,840 --> 00:01:33,353
A change sits in a queue,

39
00:01:33,353 --> 00:01:34,745
waiting for a reviewer.

40
00:01:34,745 --> 00:01:36,499
Then waiting for an approval.

41
00:01:36,499 --> 00:01:38,859
Then waiting for a free slot to deploy.

42
00:01:39,409 --> 00:01:41,693
So measure two things for every step.

43
00:01:41,693 --> 00:01:44,595
Work time: someone is actually doing something.

44
00:01:44,595 --> 00:01:47,558
And wait time: the change is just sitting there.

45
00:01:48,108 --> 00:01:49,104
Add up the work,

46
00:01:49,104 --> 00:01:50,349
divide by the total.

47
00:01:50,349 --> 00:01:51,843
That is flow efficiency.

48
00:01:51,843 --> 00:01:54,520
Many teams find it is under 20 percent.

49
00:01:54,520 --> 00:01:56,200
Eighty percent of the time,

50
00:01:56,200 --> 00:01:57,196
nothing happens.

51
00:01:57,746 --> 00:02:02,551
Drawing every step with its work and its waiting is called a value stream map.

52
00:02:02,551 --> 00:02:03,721
It is just a table,

53
00:02:03,721 --> 00:02:04,522
or a picture,

54
00:02:04,522 --> 00:02:05,938
of where the time goes.

55
00:02:07,488 --> 00:02:09,045
How do you find the bottleneck?

56
00:02:09,595 --> 00:02:12,689
Step one: list every step, from commit to production.

57
00:02:13,239 --> 00:02:15,366
Step two: measure the time in each,

58
00:02:15,366 --> 00:02:16,400
work and waiting.

59
00:02:16,400 --> 00:02:18,163
Use the data from your tools,

60
00:02:18,163 --> 00:02:18,892
not guesses.

61
00:02:19,442 --> 00:02:23,453
Step three: find the step with the biggest time. That is your candidate.

62
00:02:24,003 --> 00:02:27,006
And a few suspects show up again and again.

63
00:02:27,006 --> 00:02:27,914
Slow reviews.

64
00:02:27,914 --> 00:02:29,102
Manual approvals.

65
00:02:29,102 --> 00:02:30,499
Slow or flaky tests.

66
00:02:30,499 --> 00:02:32,385
Waiting for an environment.

67
00:02:32,385 --> 00:02:33,502
And big batches,

68
00:02:33,502 --> 00:02:36,227
where changes wait until a release day.

69
00:02:36,777 --> 00:02:40,467
Notice something about those. Most of them are waiting, not working.

70
00:02:42,017 --> 00:02:44,193
Now the fix. And there are three rules.

71
00:02:44,743 --> 00:02:49,244
One: fix one bottleneck at a time. Change one thing, so you know what helped.

72
00:02:49,794 --> 00:02:51,591
Two: measure again afterwards.

73
00:02:51,591 --> 00:02:53,567
The numbers from the last lesson,

74
00:02:53,567 --> 00:02:55,004
lead time and frequency,

75
00:02:55,004 --> 00:02:56,920
will tell you whether it worked.

76
00:02:57,470 --> 00:02:59,889
Three: expect the bottleneck to move.

77
00:02:59,889 --> 00:03:01,981
When the slowest step gets fast,

78
00:03:01,981 --> 00:03:04,139
another step becomes the slowest.

79
00:03:04,139 --> 00:03:05,446
That is not failure.

80
00:03:05,446 --> 00:03:06,558
That is progress.

81
00:03:07,108 --> 00:03:11,374
Then you repeat. Find, fix, measure. It is a loop, and it never ends.

82
00:03:11,924 --> 00:03:13,123
In the next lesson,

83
00:03:13,123 --> 00:03:17,035
we put it together: a plan to improve a real delivery process,

84
00:03:17,035 --> 00:03:17,855
step by step.

85
00:03:19,405 --> 00:03:23,458
Go to the demo folder.

86
00:03:25,008 --> 00:03:26,907
Look around.

87
00:03:27,457 --> 00:03:30,017
Two files of stage times, and one script.

88
00:03:30,567 --> 00:03:34,279
The stage times.

89
00:03:34,829 --> 00:03:35,520
Six stages,

90
00:03:35,520 --> 00:03:36,775
from code to deploy.

91
00:03:36,775 --> 00:03:37,591
For each one,

92
00:03:37,591 --> 00:03:38,721
the hours of work,

93
00:03:38,721 --> 00:03:40,290
and the hours of waiting.

94
00:03:40,840 --> 00:03:46,664
Find the bottleneck.

95
00:03:47,214 --> 00:03:49,390
Each bar is the time in one stage.

96
00:03:49,390 --> 00:03:51,630
The longest bar is clear: approval.

97
00:03:51,630 --> 00:03:53,166
30.5 hours,

98
00:03:53,166 --> 00:03:55,214
44 percent of the total.

99
00:03:55,764 --> 00:03:56,955
Look at the bottom.

100
00:03:56,955 --> 00:03:58,397
70 hours in total,

101
00:03:58,397 --> 00:04:00,779
but only 10.5 hours of work.

102
00:04:00,779 --> 00:04:02,847
Flow efficiency: 15 percent.

103
00:04:03,397 --> 00:04:05,133
Notice the review is long too.

104
00:04:05,133 --> 00:04:07,796
But fixing the review first would barely help,

105
00:04:07,796 --> 00:04:09,648
while approval holds everything.

106
00:04:10,198 --> 00:04:17,280
After the fix.

107
00:04:17,830 --> 00:04:22,822
Approval fell to 2.5 hours. The total fell from 70 hours to 26.

108
00:04:23,372 --> 00:04:25,942
And look: the slowest stage is now code.

109
00:04:25,942 --> 00:04:27,290
The bottleneck moved.

110
00:04:27,290 --> 00:04:29,282
Flow efficiency: 40 percent.

111
00:04:29,832 --> 00:04:33,842
Write it down.

112
00:04:39,192 --> 00:04:41,582
Check the file.

113
00:04:43,132 --> 00:04:46,353
Your practice task: find a bottleneck, and watch it move.

114
00:04:46,903 --> 00:04:50,253
One: look at the stage times, and guess the slowest stage.

115
00:04:50,803 --> 00:04:52,915
Two: run the script, and check your guess.

116
00:04:53,465 --> 00:04:57,262
Three: run it on the after file, and find the new slowest stage.

117
00:04:57,812 --> 00:04:59,391
Four: write your three lines.

118
00:05:01,441 --> 00:05:04,662
Where to find the demo files. Pause here, and write it down.

119
00:05:05,212 --> 00:05:10,887
One: the repository, github.com/it-waves/devops-ai.

120
00:05:11,437 --> 00:05:15,554
Two: the folder academy/lesson151-demo.

121
00:05:16,104 --> 00:05:21,118
Three: two stage files, one script called bottleneck, and a read me with the answers.

122
00:05:21,668 --> 00:05:23,373
Four: clone the repository,

123
00:05:23,373 --> 00:05:24,636
and copy the folder.

124
00:05:24,636 --> 00:05:25,710
You need a shell,

125
00:05:25,710 --> 00:05:26,215
and awk,

126
00:05:26,215 --> 00:05:28,110
which every Linux machine has.

127
00:05:28,660 --> 00:05:29,396
Five: again,

128
00:05:29,396 --> 00:05:30,745
no lab in this domain.

129
00:05:30,745 --> 00:05:32,093
The practice is plain,

130
00:05:32,093 --> 00:05:34,484
and the answers are on the next screen.

131
00:05:36,534 --> 00:05:39,009
Check yourself. Six points. Tick each one.

132
00:05:39,559 --> 00:05:42,268
One: the slowest stage at the start is approval.

133
00:05:42,818 --> 00:05:44,973
Two: the total time is 70 hours.

134
00:05:45,523 --> 00:05:47,891
Three: flow efficiency is 15 percent.

135
00:05:48,441 --> 00:05:51,641
Four: after the fix, the total is 26 hours.

136
00:05:52,191 --> 00:05:55,860
Five: the slowest stage is now code. The bottleneck moved.

137
00:05:56,410 --> 00:05:59,290
Six: your file has three lines, in your own words.

138
00:05:59,840 --> 00:06:01,995
All six, and you can find a bottleneck.

139
00:06:04,045 --> 00:06:05,816
Your turn, on your own machine.

140
00:06:06,366 --> 00:06:08,200
One: copy the demo folder.

141
00:06:08,750 --> 00:06:10,734
Two: run the script on both files.

142
00:06:11,284 --> 00:06:13,712
Three: edit one number in the first file,

143
00:06:13,712 --> 00:06:14,718
and run it again.

144
00:06:14,718 --> 00:06:16,554
Watch the slowest stage change.

145
00:06:17,104 --> 00:06:20,048
Four: write your three lines, and tick the six points.

146
00:06:20,598 --> 00:06:24,523
Five: then think about your own process. Which step would you measure first?

147
00:06:25,073 --> 00:06:26,908
